body:
Running the following code in the console on either `https://example.com` or `about:blank`:

```js
window.onerror = () => { console.log("window onerror"); };

try {
  location.href = "file:///x/";
} catch (e) {
  console.log("caught");
}
```

produces the following results:

- Safari, Chrome: a console error, but neither of the `console.log()`s are hit. Stays on the original page.
- Firefox: logs `caught`. Stays on the original page.

Running similar code but with `location.href = "./x"`, and from a `file:` URL, produces:

- Safari: a console error, but neither of the `console.log()`s are hit. Stays on the original page.
- Firefox, Chrome: navigates to the `x` file

In Chrome and probably Safari, this disallowing is synchronous. However sync vs. async is not really observable today. It will become semi-observable with [app history](https://wicg.github.io/app-history/) in that `appHistory.navigate("file:///x/")` would reject after one microtask if the disallowing is sync, and reject sometime later if the disallowing is async.

In the spec today attempting to navigate to a `file:` URL will hit "process a navigate fetch" (since `file:` is a fetch scheme). [Fetch](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#scheme-fetch) then leaves the result as an exercise to the reader. So you could get async disallowing in the current spec if the browsers decided to return a 204 for all navigation-triggered file URL fetches.

My proposal:

- Synchronously disallow navigations from non-`file:` URLs to `file:` URLs, with no exception. (Firefox would change to remove their exception-throwing behavior.)
- Allow navigations to `file:` URLs from `file:` URLs. (Safari would change to allow this.)
